      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the effect of the Vinyl Acetate (VA) content on EVA hot melt adhesives?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Ethylene-Vinyl Acetate (EVA) base copolymers are characterized by the ratio of ethylene (crystalline block) to vinyl acetate (amorphous block). Increasing the VA content reduces the polymer's crystallinity, which lowers its melting point, increases flexibility, extends the open time, and enhances adhesion to polar substrates. Conversely, decreasing the VA content increases crystallinity, providing higher tensile strength, faster set speeds, higher heat resistance, and better cohesion.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How do tackifiers modify the surface wetting properties of hot melts?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Tackifying resins are low molecular weight amorphous polymers that are compatible with the base polymer. They function by reducing the glass transition temperature (Tg) and the molten surface energy of the adhesive blend. This reduction enables the molten hot melt to rapidly wet the substrate surface and flow into its microscopic pores under light application pressure, developing high polar adhesion upon contact.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What role do crystalline waxes play in hot melt set speed?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Waxes are highly crystalline, low molecular weight hydrocarbons that serve as viscosity reducers and set speed regulators. When the hot melt cools, the wax nucleates and crystallizes rapidly. This rapid phase change from a low-viscosity liquid to a highly structured crystalline solid acts as a physical cross-link, locking the polymer chains together and giving the adhesive its ultimate bond strength within seconds.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Why are antioxidants critical in hot melt formulations?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Hot melt adhesives are held in molten reservoirs at high temperatures (150-180°C) for hours or days before application. Under these conditions, atmospheric oxygen triggers thermal-oxidative degradation of the polymer chains, leading to chain scission (loss of viscosity/cohesion) or polymer cross-linking (gelation, charring, skinning, and nozzle clogging). Hindered phenols and phosphite antioxidants are incorporated to scavenge free radicals and decompose hydroperoxides, stabilizing the melt.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How does 'open time' differ from 'setting time' in hot melt adhesives?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              'Open time' refers to the maximum time window after application during which the adhesive remains sufficiently molten and tacky to wet and bond to a second substrate. 'Setting time' (or set speed) is the time required for the assembled joint to cool down and develop sufficient cohesive strength to hold the substrates together without mechanical clamping force.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What are the primary differences between metallocene polyolefins and traditional EVA hot melts?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Metallocene polyolefins are synthesized using single-site metallocene catalysts, yielding polymers with highly uniform molecular weight distributions and precise monomer sequencing. Compared to EVA, metallocene polyolefin hot melts exhibit significantly higher thermal stability (no thermal charring or vinyl odor), excellent low-temperature flexibility, lower application temperatures, and high shear resistance at reduced coat weights.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
